.\" Manpage for govenv.
.\" Keep in sync with the --help output in main.go and the README.
.TH GOVENV 1 "2026-06-04" "govenv 0.1.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
govenv \- per-project Go environments
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B govenv
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIENV_DIR\fR
.br
.B govenv
\fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B govenv
automates per-project environment setup for Go modules, in the spirit of
Python's \fBvenv\fR. Run from a Go module directory, it builds the module's
binary into a dedicated environment directory and writes an
.I activate
script that puts that binary (and optionally a pinned Go toolchain) at the
front of your
.BR PATH .
.PP
There is no daemon, cache, or global state \(em just a directory containing a
binary and a shell script.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list\-go
List Go toolchains detected under
.IR ~/sdk/ ,
where the Go team's downloader installs versioned toolchains.
.B govenv
detects them but does not install them.
.TP
.B deactivate
Scripting fallback that prints shell code to restore
.B PATH
and unset the govenv variables. To exit an env interactively you do not need
this \(em use the
.B govenv\-deactivate
command described in
.B ACTIVATION
below. Because a binary cannot modify the shell that launched it, this
subcommand only prints the commands; eval them in the current shell:
\fBeval \(dq$(govenv deactivate)\(dq\fR.
.TP
.B version
Print the govenv version (also
.B \-v
/
.BR \-\-version ).
For binaries built with
.B go install
this is the module tag; for a local build it is the VCS revision.
.TP
.B help
Show usage. Each command also accepts
.B \-h
/
.B \-\-help
for command-specific help.
.SH OPTIONS
These apply to the default create/manage command
.RB ( "govenv [options] ENV_DIR" ).
.TP
.B \-\-clear
Delete the environment directory before creating it.
.TP
.B \-\-upgrade
Rebuild and reinstall the binary into an existing environment.
.TP
.BI \-\-go " VERSION"
Use a specific Go toolchain installed under
.IR ~/sdk/ ", e.g. " 1.22.3 .
.TP
.BI \-\-prompt " NAME"
Override the prompt prefix shown on activation (defaults to the module's
binary name).
.TP
.B \-\-symlinks
Symlink the binary into the environment directory instead of copying.
.TP
.B \-\-copies
Copy the binary into the environment directory (default).
.SH ACTIVATION
Activate an environment by sourcing its
.I activate
script in your current shell:
.PP
.RS
.nf
source .venv/activate
.fi
.RE
.PP
This prepends the environment to
.BR PATH ,
sets the
.B GOVENV
variables, and defines a shell function named
.B govenv\-deactivate
in your shell. To exit the environment, run that command:
.PP
.RS
.nf
govenv\-deactivate
.fi
.RE
.PP
It restores
.BR PATH ,
unsets the govenv variables, and removes itself. The name is deliberately
distinct from Python venv's
.B deactivate
so the two never shadow each other when both are active in the same shell.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
While an environment is active, the
.I activate
script sets:
.TP
.B GOVENV
Absolute path of the active environment directory.
.TP
.B GOVENV_PROMPT
Prompt label shown on activation.
.TP
.B GOVENV_GO_ROOT
Root of the pinned Go toolchain (empty unless
.B \-\-go
was used).
.TP
.B _GOVENV_OLD_PATH
Saved
.B PATH
from before activation;
.B govenv\-deactivate
restores it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create an environment and activate it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
govenv .venv
source .venv/activate
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pin a Go toolchain:
.PP
.RS
.nf
go install golang.org/dl/go1.22.3@latest && go1.22.3 download
govenv \-\-go 1.22.3 .venv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Rebuild after code changes, then exit:
.PP
.RS
.nf
govenv \-\-upgrade .venv
govenv\-deactivate
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR go (1)
.PP
Project home: https://github.com/kenk667/govenv
.SH AUTHOR
Ken K.
